\section{Version 2}{

\code{version = 2} lists three more cases exactly, which version 1 flags, and
adds the tables they need. A reader that knows only version 1 must not be
handed a version-2 list: their records are no longer flagged.
\itemize{
\item Gradient fills: linear and radial gradients with \code{pad} extension.
\code{paints$fill_gradient} is the 1-based row of \code{gradients} (0 = none; then
\code{fill} is \code{NA}). \code{gradients} has \code{type} (\code{"linear"} or \code{"radial"}), the
geometry in the shape's local px (linear: \verb{x1, y1} to \verb{x2, y2}; radial:
the focal circle \verb{x1, y1, r1} at offset 0 to the outer circle \verb{x2, y2, r2}
at offset 1), the transform placing it in device px as \code{a}, \code{b}, \code{c},
\code{d}, \code{e}, \code{f} (the SVG / canvas \code{matrix(a, b, c, d, e, f)}), and its stops:
gradient \emph{i}'s are rows \code{stop_offset[i] + 1} to \code{stop_offset[i + 1]} of
\code{stop_pos} (0 to 1) and \code{stop_color} (\code{"#rrggbbaa"}).
\item Path clips: a clip by a path or a rotated rectangle. \code{clips} gains
\code{path_start} and \code{path_n}: a clip's paths are shapes \code{path_start + 1} to
\code{path_start + path_n} of \code{clip_paths} (0 = the rect alone), and a record is
clipped to the rect and to each of them. Its rect is then also bounded by
the paths' boxes. \code{clip_paths} is a path table like \code{path}: \code{xy},
\code{run_offset}, \code{path_offset} (one shape per entry) and \code{evenodd}.
\item Opacity groups: a group whose only effect is an opacity below 1.
\code{record$group} is the 1-based row of \code{groups} (the innermost such group
the record is drawn in, 0 = none). \code{groups} has \code{alpha} and \code{parent}
(1-based, 0 = top level). A client draws a group's records into their own
layer and composites it at \code{alpha}.
}

Masks, blend modes, blur and shadow effects, hatches, patterns, repeating or
reflecting gradients and gradient strokes stay flagged.
\itemize{
\item Flagged runs (VW-30): \code{record$run} is the run of flagged marks a record
belongs to (1-based, 0 = none) and \code{runs} their count. A run is a stretch
of flagged marks in paint order that no exactly drawn mark interrupts;
every record of a node with a flagged record, and of an opacity group
holding one, is in it. \code{scene_png(layer = "keyed_flagged", run = k)}
draws run \code{k}, so a client can draw the exact records itself and
composite each run's raster where the run starts.
\item Blend modes in runs (VW-32): \code{run_blend} gives, per run, how its raster
composites onto what is beneath it: \code{"normal"}, or a CSS \code{mix-blend-mode}
keyword (\code{"multiply"}, \code{"screen"}, ...) when the run is exactly the marks
of one group with that blend mode drawn onto the page, directly or
inside groups with no opacity, mask, blend or effect, which isolate
nothing (a \emph{blend root}; a run ends where the blend root changes).
Rendered on its own, such a run's raster is its marks unblended, and
compositing it with that mode reproduces the blend. A blend nested inside
a mask, an effect or an opacity group happens within that group's raster,
so its run is \code{"normal"}. \code{""} would mark a run that cannot be composited
from its raster; current lists have none.
}
}

\section{What stage 1 does not reproduce}{

Such marks are still listed, so they can be hit-tested, but \code{flags} marks
them and a client should fall back to its approximate path for them:
\code{1} a gradient, pattern or hatch paint (the paint's colour is \code{NA}); \code{2} a
clip that is not an axis-aligned rectangle (the clip is not listed); \code{4} a
mark inside a group with opacity, blend mode, mask or effect; \code{8} a text
label (the geometry is its box); \code{16} pick-only: a keyed mark that paints
nothing (an invisible hover target such as a \code{fill = NA} rect, circle,
marker or polygon). Its geometry is listed and its paint has \code{fill} and
\code{stroke} \code{NA}, so a client hit-tests it but draws nothing; \code{32} inside a
group with a blend mode (with \code{4}): the mark mixes with what is painted
beneath it, so its run's raster reproduces it only when composited with
the run's \code{run_blend} mode. Images do not appear at all.
}

